.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1258 AM PDT Sat Sep 5 2026
(Today and tonight)

On Thursday the presence of mid to upper level troughing and a
surface cool front brought many (but not all locations) an early
rain, in some cases an early September daily record breaking rain.
Atypical cool air aloft for early September carried over to Friday
and plenty of cool air aloft is still lingering at this hour early
Saturday morning. With current temperatures broadly in the 50s/60s,
coolest at higher elevations, it`s relief e.g. for a time of year
here when it can easily be much warmer at higher elevations in the
lower level temperature inversion under high pressure aloft. Mountain
top air temperatures from Mt St Helena all the way to Chews Ridge
are currently in the lower 50s. Mt Hamilton 30 year early Sep normal
low for example is 62F.

Recently sea surface temperatures in our coastal waters have bumped
upwards to the lower to mid 60s north to south, now 2F to 5F above
Sep normals. This is resulting in a warming effect on nearby coastal
locations where daily minimums are remaining a few to several degrees
warmer than normal. The usual night/morning chill may be noticeably
absent in the last few days.

If you`re enjoying the daytime temperatures from the coast to far
inland, daytime highs today will be similar to maybe a few degrees
warmer with 60s/70s coastside/bayside to the upper 70s to lower 80s
inland.

